---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Doc from '../components/legal/Doc.astro';
const updated = '29 October 2026';
// Drafts stay out of search until counsel signs off and every placeholder is filled (design/legal/README.md).
const draft = true;
---

<Base title="Creator agreement · Comarket" description="The agreement for creators on Comarket: how sales count, when you get paid, taxes, disclosure rules, your content and your right to turn brands down." stage="none" noindex={draft}>
  <Doc
    title="Creator agreement"
    lede="Comarket is free for creators. You get paid on every sale you send. This is the agreement behind that, in full."
    updated={updated}
    draft={draft}
    current="/creator-terms"
  >
    <div class="short">
      <b>The short version</b>
      <ul>
        <li>You never pay Comarket. Brands pay us.</li>
        <li>You earn the rate you accepted on each completed sale from a buyer new to the brand, and on their renewals for 12 months.</li>
        <li>We pay you monthly through Stripe, once the brand's refund window has closed and the brand has paid us.</li>
        <li>Say clearly, in every post, that you earn from the link. Never buy through your own link or pay people to buy.</li>
        <li>You can turn down any brand or offer, and stop promoting one, whenever you like.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>1. About this agreement</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">1.1</span>This Creator Agreement is between [[LEGAL ENTITY NAME]] ("Comarket", "we", "us") and you, the
      person or business that accepts it when opening a creator account. It includes the general terms in
      <a href="/terms#part-b-general-terms">Part B of the Terms of use</a> and the <a href="/acceptable-use">acceptable use
      policy</a>. If they conflict, this agreement applies first, then the acceptable use policy, then Part B.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">1.2</span>To join you must be at least 18, publish content under your own name or brand, and be able to
      receive payouts through Stripe in your country. You join for your creator work, as an independent business, not as a
      consumer. If you accept for a company, you confirm that you can bind it. Capitalised words not defined here,
      such as Qualifying Sale, Commission Period, Net Amount, Refund Window and Offer, have the meanings in section 2 of the
      <a href="/brand-terms">Brand Agreement</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Your page and storefronts</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">2.1</span>You get a Hub at comarket.si/your-handle and, for each Offer you accept and the brand approves, a
      Storefront in that brand's look with your name on it. You may keep using other link pages and add your Comarket links to
      them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">2.2</span>Your handle is yours to use while your account is open. We may refuse or withdraw a handle that
      impersonates someone, uses a name or trademark you have no right to, or is offensive or misleading, and we will offer you
      another. When an account closes, we keep its handle unused for 90 days before anyone else can claim it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">2.3</span>Comarket is free for you. We don't charge creators any fee, and we pay Stripe's standard fees for
      sending your payouts. If you are paid in a currency other than [[BILLING CURRENCY]], Stripe or your bank may charge for
      the conversion.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Offers and your right to say no</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">3.1</span>Brands send you Offers through Comarket. Each Offer shows the product, what buyers get, your rate,
      the Refund Window, where you may promote it, and the claims you may make about it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">3.2</span>You may accept or decline any Offer, without a reason. Declining never counts against you in
      matching. A Storefront goes live only after you accept and the brand approves you.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">3.3</span>You may stop promoting an Offer at any time and ask us to take its Storefront down. We do so within
      2 business days. A brand may also withdraw its approval, and we will tell you. Sales from checkouts started before a
      Storefront came down still count.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">3.4</span><b>How we match.</b> We suggest Offers to you, and you to brands, mainly on: how closely your topic
      and audience fit the brand's product; where your audience is; your platforms and formats; your past results on
      Comarket, such as completed sales and refund rates; whether your rate fits the Offer; your preferences; and our checks
      for quality, compliance and fraud. No one can pay for a higher place in matching.
    </p>

    <h2>4. What counts as a sale</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">4.1</span>You earn on a Qualifying Sale: a completed payment by a buyer who is new to the brand and who
      started the checkout from your Storefront, within the attribution window, that isn't refunded within its Refund Window
      and isn't excluded. Section 6 of the <a href="/brand-terms">Brand Agreement</a> sets out the test in full; it applies to
      you as written there.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">4.2</span>We check whether a buyer is new by comparing their email, phone number and card fingerprint with
      the brand's existing Stripe customers. If any of them matches, the sale doesn't count.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">4.3</span>Your dashboard shows every sale and, when one doesn't count, why. It never shows who the buyer
      was. You can ask for a person to review any decision (section 8).
    </p>

    <h2>5. Your earnings</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.1</span>For each Qualifying Sale you earn the rate in the Offer you accepted, on the First Payment and on
      each Renewal during the Commission Period (12 months from the First Payment), unless the Offer says it pays on the First
      Payment only.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.2</span>Earnings on a payment are confirmed when its Refund Window closes without a full refund. A partial
      refund reduces them in proportion.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.3</span><b>Who pays you.</b> The brand owes you your earnings. You appoint Comarket as your limited agent to
      collect them from the brand and pay them to you. When the brand pays us, the brand's debt to you for that amount is
      settled, and we owe it to you instead.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.4</span><b>When.</b> On or about day [[PAYOUT DAY]] of each month, we pay all your confirmed earnings that
      the brand has paid us, if they total at least [[MINIMUM PAYOUT]]. Smaller amounts carry over to the next month. If you
      close your account, we pay any balance at the next payout date, whatever its size.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.5</span><b>If a brand pays late.</b> We pay you at the first payout date after the brand pays us. We chase
      late brands and tell you when your earnings are waiting on one. If a brand doesn't pay, we are not obliged to pay you
      amounts we haven't received, but we will take reasonable steps to recover them, and we will pass on anything we recover.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.6</span><b>Stripe.</b> Payouts go to your bank through a Stripe connected account. You need to accept
      Stripe's Connected Account Agreement, give Stripe the identity details it asks for, and keep the account in good
      standing. We can't pay you while Stripe has restricted your account.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.7</span><b>Reversals after payout.</b> If, after we have paid you, the buyer wins a chargeback within
      [[CHARGEBACK PERIOD]] days of the payment, or we find that the sale should be excluded (for example as fraud), we deduct
      the amount from your future earnings. We ask you to repay it directly only if the sale came from your own breach of this
      agreement, or if no further earnings are due within 6 months.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.8</span><b>Mistakes.</b> If we pay you too much or too little by mistake, we correct it at the next payout
      and show the correction.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Taxes</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">6.1</span>You are responsible for the income tax, social contributions, VAT, GST and other taxes on your
      earnings, and for registering where the law requires.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">6.2</span>Before your first payout, you give us, through Stripe or Comarket, the tax information we
      request: in the US, a Form W-9 if you are a US person, or a Form W-8BEN or W-8BEN-E if you are not. We, or Stripe for us,
      may issue the information returns US law requires, such as Form 1099-NEC or 1099-K.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">6.3</span>Laws such as the EU's DAC7 rules may require us to collect your name, address, date of birth, tax
      identification number, VAT number and bank details, and to report your earnings to tax authorities. We will do that only
      as the law requires, and we will tell you what we reported.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">6.4</span><b>Invoices.</b> If you are registered for VAT or GST, give us your number and tell us if it
      changes. You authorise us to issue invoices or statements for your earnings on your behalf, in the form the law allows,
      and you agree not to issue your own invoices for the same amounts. If tax law treats these as self-billed invoices, you
      accept them as such, and you may object to any one within 30 days.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">6.5</span>We may withhold tax from payouts where the law requires it, and we will show what we withheld. We
      don't give tax advice.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Disclosing that you earn</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.1</span>Every time you promote an Offer, in a post, story, video, livestream, newsletter, podcast,
      comment or message, disclose your connection to the brand in a way your audience can't miss: that the link is paid,
      that you earn a commission, or that it's an ad.
    </p>
    <p><span class="n">7.2</span>That means:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>put the disclosure at the start, in the same language as the post, not hidden in hashtags, behind "more" or only in your bio;</li>
      <li>in video and audio, say it out loud as well as on screen, near the start;</li>
      <li>use the platform's paid-partnership tools where they exist, as well as your own words, not instead of them;</li>
      <li>use plain words such as "Ad", "Paid link" or "I earn a commission if you buy". Words like "sp", "collab" or "thanks to" on their own are not enough.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.3</span>These duties come from, among others: in the US, the FTC's Guides Concerning the Use of Endorsements
      and Testimonials in Advertising (16 CFR Part 255) and its rule on consumer reviews and testimonials (16 CFR Part 465);
      in the UK, the CAP Code (including its rule that ads must be obviously identifiable) and the consumer protection law the
      CMA enforces; and in the EU, the Unfair Commercial Practices Directive and the national laws and advertising codes that
      implement it. Follow the rules of the country your audience is in, and the platform's own rules.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.4</span><b>Be honest.</b> Only say what you really think. Don't say you use a product unless you do. Make
      only the claims the Offer allows about what the product does, its price and its results, and never claims about health,
      money or results that the Offer doesn't include.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.5</span>Every Storefront also shows that you earn a commission. Don't hide or cover it.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Questioning a payout or a decision</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">8.1</span>You may question any sale, earning or payout within 60 days of it appearing in your dashboard, by
      writing to [[CONTACT EMAIL]]. A person at Comarket reviews it, may ask you and the brand for information, and tells you
      the decision and the reasons within 30 days.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">8.2</span>If you still disagree, the complaints and mediation process in Part B applies.
    </p>

    <h2>9. What you must not do</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">9.1</span>The <a href="/acceptable-use">acceptable use policy</a> sets out the rules in full. In particular,
      you must not:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>buy through your own link,</b> or have someone in your household, or anyone acting for you, do so;</li>
      <li><b>pay or reward buyers</b> to buy, for example by sharing your commission, offering cashback or running a giveaway that requires a purchase, unless the Offer allows it;</li>
      <li><b>stuff cookies or force clicks,</b> for example with hidden frames, pop-unders, auto-redirects, browser extensions or adware;</li>
      <li><b>leak codes or links</b> to coupon, cashback or deal sites, unless the Offer allows it;</li>
      <li><b>spam:</b> send unsolicited email, texts or direct messages, or post links in places that forbid them, in breach of laws such as CAN-SPAM, the UK's PECR or the EU ePrivacy rules;</li>
      <li><b>bid on a brand's name</b> or trademarks in paid search, or use them in a domain or account name, without the brand's written permission;</li>
      <li><b>pose as the brand,</b> its staff or its official account, or as an ordinary customer;</li>
      <li><b>make misleading claims</b> or post fake or paid-for reviews;</li>
      <li><b>use bots,</b> fake followers or bought engagement, or misstate your audience;</li>
      <li><b>target children,</b> or promote an Offer in content made for children; or</li>
      <li><b>get around the new-customer check,</b> for example with made-up identities or several accounts.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <span class="n">9.2</span>If a sale comes from any of these, it doesn't count, and we may withhold or reverse your earnings on
      the sales affected. For a serious or repeated breach, we may also suspend or end your account under section 12.
    </p>

    <h2>10. Your content</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">10.1</span>You own the content you create, and your name, likeness and handle remain yours.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">10.2</span>You give Comarket a non-exclusive, worldwide, royalty-free licence, while your account is open and
      for the time needed to take things down after, to host, copy, display and adapt for layout (such as resizing, cropping or
      reformatting) your name, handle, photo, bio, public profile statistics and the content you add or link, in order to run
      your Hub and Storefronts, show your profile and results to brands you are matched with, and run Comarket. We may
      sublicense this only to the brand for its Storefronts with you, and to our service providers.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">10.3</span>We feature you or your content in Comarket's own marketing only if you agree to it separately.
      A brand may use your content outside its Storefronts, for example in its own ads, only if you agree through an Offer or
      in writing.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">10.4</span>You confirm that you have the rights to the content you add, that anyone shown in it has agreed,
      and that it doesn't infringe anyone's rights or break the law. To the extent the law allows, you agree not to assert
      moral rights against formatting changes we make for layout.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">10.5</span>You may use a brand's names, logos and materials only to promote its Offer, as the Offer allows,
      while you are approved, and in line with any brand guidelines in the Offer.
    </p>

    <h2>11. Data</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">11.1</span>Our <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> explains how we handle your information, including what
      brands see about you.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">11.2</span>We never give you buyers' identities or contact details. If a buyer contacts you directly, you are
      responsible for handling their information lawfully. Don't send buyers' personal information to Comarket.
    </p>

    <h2>12. Suspension and ending</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">12.1</span>You may close your account at any time from your settings or by writing to [[CONTACT EMAIL]]. We
      take your Hub and Storefronts down within 2 business days.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">12.2</span>We may end this agreement by giving you 30 days' notice, with our reasons. We may suspend your
      account, or end the agreement sooner, with our reasons, for a serious or repeated breach of this agreement or the
      acceptable use policy, for fraud, or if the law or a regulator requires it. We will tell you the reasons when the
      suspension or ending starts, unless the law or the risk of fraud prevents that, and you can respond through the
      complaints process in Part B.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">12.3</span><b>Earnings after you leave.</b> If the agreement ends for any reason other than your fraud or
      serious breach, you keep earning on Qualifying Sales made before it ended, including their Renewals until each Commission
      Period ends, and we keep paying you monthly under section 5. If it ends for your fraud or serious breach, we pay only the
      confirmed earnings that the breach didn't affect.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">12.4</span>You don't have to delete posts you published before the end, but you must remove or stop sharing
      Comarket links that no longer work if we ask, and you may no longer use brands' materials.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">12.5</span>Sections 5 to 8, 9.2, 10.4, 12.3 to 12.5, 13 and 14, and Part B, survive the end of this agreement.
    </p>

    <h2>13. Liability</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">13.1</span>You will compensate Comarket for losses, including fines and reasonable legal costs, caused by
      third-party claims to the extent they arise from your content, your missing or unclear disclosures, your breach of
      section 9 or the acceptable use policy, or your taxes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">13.2</span>Neither party is liable for lost profits, revenue, business or goodwill, or for indirect or
      consequential loss, arising from this agreement. Our total liability to you arising from this agreement in any
      12-month period is limited to the greater of the amounts paid and payable to you in the 12 months before the event
      giving rise to the claim, and [[CREATOR LIABILITY FLOOR]].
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">13.3</span>No limit in this section applies to our duty to pay you earnings we have received for you, to
      fraud, gross negligence or wilful misconduct, to death or personal injury caused by negligence, or to any liability that
      can't be limited by law.
    </p>

    <h2>14. Other terms</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">14.1</span>You are an independent business, not our employee, worker or agent, and you decide how and when you
      create. Nothing here stops you working with other brands or platforms, unless you accept an Offer that asks for
      exclusivity in a category for a stated time.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">14.2</span>We may change this agreement under section 13 of Part B: at least 15 days' notice, and you may
      leave before a change applies.
    </p>
  </Doc>
